<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # One Step In, Two Steps Out! – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> Kinder (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Let's Dance!</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> One Step In, Two Steps Out!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Engage with simple commands using basic words and gestures.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can recite simple action rhymes (e.g., 'head, shoulders, knees, and toes').
            Can give very simple commands to peers (e.g., says 'head,' point to head).
            Can participate in conversations about movements, giving simple commands (e.g., 'shake' or 'turn').</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f0f8ff;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 34%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task (Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher activates prior knowledge and introduces the lesson's focus through engaging activities, clear modeling, and concept checking.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads a "Move Your Body" activity. Students stand in a circle. The teacher calls out familiar body parts (e.g., "Head!", "Shoulders!", "Knees!", "Toes!") and students point and say the word. Then, introduce simple movements like "Shake your hands!" or "Hop, hop, hop!" linking to the theme. Ask, "Are we moving fast or slow today?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher demonstrates simple dance commands using clear gestures and a slow pace. For example, "One step IN!" (takes one step forward into the circle) and "Two steps OUT!" (takes two steps backward out of the circle). The teacher emphasizes the pronunciation of /t/ in 'two' and /d/ in 'dance'. The teacher also demonstrates "Turn around!" and "Shake your body!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Demonstrating one step) "Am I taking one step or two steps?" (Expected student response: "One step!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Stepping forward) "Am I going IN or OUT?" (Expected student response: "IN!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Spinning) "Am I jumping or spinning?" (Expected student response: "Spinning!")</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">New language or content is introduced in a meaningful context, connected to the scenario.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario "Let's Dance!":</b> The teacher plays a simple "Dance Command Song" that incorporates the target vocabulary and grammar. The song includes phrases like "First, one step in!", "Next, two steps out!", "Shake your hands!", "Turn around!", "Hop like a bunny!", "Jump over the line!", "Move next to the chair!", "Dance fast! Dance slow!". The song also highlights sounds like /J/ in 'jump' and /S/ in 'step'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> "Let's Dance!" Action Song (audio track with visual aids showing children performing the actions or flashcards illustrating each command).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Acting out dance commands, reciting action rhymes.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question:</b> "What are we doing in the song? Are we sleeping or dancing?" (Students respond "Dancing!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>TPR Response:</b> "Show me 'shake'!" (Students shake their bodies). "Show me 'jump'!" (Students jump).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (Gesture-based):</b> Teacher performs an action, "I am jumping fast." If true, students give thumbs up; if false, thumbs down. (e.g., teacher slowly jumps – thumbs down; teacher jumps fast – thumbs up).</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students practice the target language with a focus on accuracy in controlled and semi-controlled activities, leading to more independent interaction.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content (Speaking Accuracy):</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar:</b> Sequential commands with numbers (e.g., 'First, take one step to the side.' 'Next, take two steps to the side.'), Simple directive phrases (e.g., 'Move to the front.' 'Step to the side.'), Positional commands with prepositions (e.g., 'Stand next to the chair.' 'Jump over the line.').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> One to ten, step, dance, move, spin, turn, twirl, shake, hop, put, jump, fast, slow, in, out, around, up, next to, over, chair, line.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> Clear pronunciation of /t/ in 'two' and /d/ in 'dance'. Recognizing initial sounds 'J' in 'jump', 'S' in 'step', 'T' in 'turn', 'U' in 'up', 'V' in 'move'.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pragmatic:</b> Acting out dance commands.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Sociolinguistic:</b> Group participation, using 'after you', 'go ahead'.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice – Echo Dance:</b> The teacher calls out commands (e.g., "First, one step in!", "Next, two steps out!", "Shake your hands fast!"), and the whole class echoes the command and performs the action. Teacher explicitly models and praises clear pronunciation of /t/ and /d/. Repeat with various commands: "Turn around slow!", "Jump over the line!", "Move next to the chair!"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice – Command Relay:</b> Students are divided into small groups. The teacher shows a flashcard (e.g., "jump" or "three") and says part of a command (e.g., "Let's ___ fast!"). A student from each group completes the command (e.g., "Let's jump fast!") and performs it. Then, the next student says the next command in the sequence (e.g., "Next, two steps back!"). Teacher provides sentence frames as needed (e.g., "___ (number) steps ___ (direction)").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction – "My Turn, Your Turn" Dance:</b> Students work in pairs. One student gives a simple command using words and gestures (e.g., "Shake!"), and the partner performs it. Then, they switch roles. Teacher encourages polite expressions like "After you!" or "Go ahead!" before taking turns. Focus on simple 1-2 word commands initially, then gradually combine with numbers and prepositions (e.g., "One step in!", "Jump over!").</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students use the language spontaneously and creatively in a realistic communicative task, focusing on fluency and task completion.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative task: "Design Our Dance!"</b> Students work in small groups (3-4 children) to create a very simple 2-3 step dance sequence using the commands they have learned. Each group will present their "dance" by having one student call out the commands while the others perform.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task format:</b> Small group role-play / presentation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme "One Step In, Two Steps Out!" and Scenario "Let's Dance!":</b> Each group will use a combination of movement commands like "First, one step in!", "Next, two steps out!", "Then, shake your hands!", "Jump around the circle!". They must use at least two sequential commands and one positional command (e.g., "Stand next to the desk.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher divides students into small groups.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group is given a set of picture cards representing different dance commands (e.g., 'jump', 'shake', 'turn', 'one step in', 'two steps out', 'next to chair').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Groups choose 2-3 cards to create their dance sequence. They practice saying the commands and performing the actions together. Teacher circulates, offering support and prompting language use.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group then presents their dance to the class. One student acts as the "dance leader" and calls out the commands, while the others perform the actions.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment measures student learning and mastery of the objective, followed by self-reflection.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Observe students during the "Design Our Dance!" activity. The teacher notes individual student's ability to give commands and follow commands within their group and during the presentation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of simple commands (e.g., 'shake', 'jump', 'one step in').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target vocabulary (movement verbs, numbers, prepositions).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of gestures accompanying commands.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Participation and collaboration in group movements.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Attempts to use polite expressions ('after you', 'go ahead').</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students will be asked to draw a happy face or a neutral face next to two simple statements written on a worksheet or projected on the board:
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can say 'jump!'"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can do 'one step in, two steps out!'"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Both students and the teacher reflect on the learning process, identifying successes and areas for improvement, and connecting to future lessons.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new dance move did you learn to say today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was your favorite dance command to tell a friend?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this lesson? (e.g., TPR activities were highly engaging, students actively participated in giving commands, pronunciation practice was effective).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? (e.g., provide more visual aids for sequential commands, encourage shy students to speak up more in pairs, reinforce the difference between 'fast' and 'slow' more explicitly).</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> In the next lesson (Lesson 4: Writing Composition), students will focus on writing. They will draw their favorite dance moves and attempt to write simple commands or label them with the new vocabulary, such as 'jump', 'shake', 'one step in', using pre-writing activities and guided sentence construction.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>